the resistance in pancheer appears to be  holding out for now but look at this  the taliban rolling through the entrance  to the valley heavily armed with  american equipment captured when the  country fell in the rugged mountainous  terrain above anti-taliban fighters  standing their grounds  the taliban failed to take this highly  symbolic territory when they last ruled  as did the soviets when they occupied  the country in the 1980s  celebratory gunfire did puncture the  night sky in kabul when taliban sources  claimed the area had fallen  but the movement's jubilation appears to  have been premature  former vice president amro lasalla quick  to deny they'd been defeated in this  clip posted on social media  the reports i've fled are propaganda he  says  there's no doubt the situation is  difficult but the resistance continues  the humanitarian situation is also  desperate  some aid arriving but it's not enough  and until the airport's fully  operational things are likely to get  much worse  in kabul taliban checkpoints command the  city where the price of food and basic  supplies is increasing all the time  people here after 40 years of war are  tired and don't know what to expect next  afghanistan is a place on the edge the  west's presence here now a ghostly  shadow and nothing sums that up better  than the abandoned jail at bagram where  the detritus of america's forever war  lies abandoned  the project to turn afghanistan into a  democracy needed huge amounts of force  and coercion  here the empty handcuffs on the wall a  reminder of how it failed  alex rossi sky news
 

